  "fanTake": "Well, that was about as convincing a loss as you could ask for—and I mean that in the worst possible way. The Patriots traveled to Seattle Wednesday night and managed to score just 10 points in what felt like a masterclass in offensive futility: we converted only 31% of our third downs (well below the 40% league average) and couldn't capitalize on our red-zone opportunities, converting just one of two trips into the end zone. The defense had some bright spots—Seattle's red-zone efficiency dropped to 33%, and their third-down conversion rate of 18% suggested we were getting stops—but we coughed the ball up three times more than we forced it back, and an offensive EPA per play of -0.11 told the real story: we were simply outmatched in Seattle's house. A.J. Brown's 6% win probability added led all Patriots players and showed what's possible when someone actually executes, but on a night when we needed the whole offense to rise up, it felt like we sent a half-strength squad to face the Seahawks.",
